\pard\plain \fi720\sl288\slmult1\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 A Solitant priest hears the word \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91white\u8217\'92 and thinks of the pure white glow of the god named Solraheen. Hrkan folk hear \u8216\'91white\u8217\'92 and think of the plain and stainless robes of Hrkan Lordship. Outland pokes hear \u8216\'91white\u8217\'92 and think of sparkling salt pans, or sun-bleached bone. As it is with white, so it is with words, they mean different things to different people. It is some wonder then, that Solraheen would choose to use such an equivocal thing as a word to create the world. He dwelt in the void, spoke one word, the world emerged, and people haven\u8217\'92t stopped arguing about the meaning of it all ever since. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 One might be led to believe that He chose to create with a word because He wanted to play with what He meant. But this is false, or so the Solitants claim. Words are not equivocal, they are fixed. Or rather, they used to be.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The word \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91art\u8217\'92, for example, originally had a clear meaning. Back when Solraheen walked the earth an artwork was nothing less than a divine message given physical form. Then evil came into the world, twisted the tongues of men, and now the heresy of art as a means of personal expression pollutes the minds of many.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 An old Solitant sermon titled \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91Art as Exegetical Instrument\u8217\'92 extracts the truth of the matter through interrogation:}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 What is art?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Who is an artist?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Who isn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 A painter, a sculptor, these are artists, yes, that\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s reasonable to say, but what about a tax collector? Is he an artist too if he plumbs the depths of greed? Is a sheep-shearer plying her shears an artist if she\u8217\'92s sufficiently clever about it? What about a farmer? Give a young man a shovel, a field of dirt, friends to impress, and what does he do? The priest leaves the derivation of the answer as an exercise to the audience. For the prude, the answer is a penis. The average Jandri boy will find it funny to dig a big old ugly in the dirt. He laughs because it doubles as a drainage ditch, just like the real thing. To place such degeneracy in the same category as the cloud of colored windows which open up the ceiling of the Gambagall\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 well! That is an affront to the sacred.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Two days ago, Vigram heard a variation of this sermon. He didn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t normally go into the temple, because that meant he\u8217\'92d have to listen to someone else speak, but he\u8217\'92d gone, and so young Mandrameen was forced to deal with the consequences.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Burly, broad-faced Vigram, with his gleaming scalp and belly beard. He smacked his rich breakfast table with a fat hand. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93The Solitants are right!\u8221\'94 Vigram roared. \u8220\'93If everyone is an artist, then the word is as useful as an unmarked weight!\u8221\'94 }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram clutched his bearded jaw and winced. The food on his table did not accommodate his toothache. This was his leech farm, and so every dish\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s principal ingredient was some variation of the Meskalimi leech. This particular leech was a prized delicacy throughout the Hrkan world as its essence was said to cure infertility, and somehow, fertility as well. The meat, if it can be called meat, was quite sweet. It was also chewy. Every bite forced Vigram\u8217\'92s tongue into acrobatics. Two bites in, it slipped, and the pain had him talking out the side of his cheek in an odd drawl. It hurt to hear the man speak, in more ways than one.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Why not choose a different word?\u8221\'94 Mandri suggested. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Because words must have walls,\u8221\'94 Vigram said as he pushed his plate away. \u8220\'93The true artist, I say this, the true artist is a divine instrument. They must be kept apart from the Self-addled horde.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri chowed down on the cold, crumbled chunks of what he supposed was boiled leech. It was quite bitter on account of the overpowering sauce, but actively trying to suppress a gag helped keep him from speaking his mind.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You know,\u8221\'94 Vigram said, getting excited, \u8220\'93the Old Jandri word for \u8216\'91artist\u8217\'92 is lost to time, but there is a word in what\u8217\'92s left of that language fit for the task\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 \u8216\'91bursheg\u8217\'92. It means something like \u8216\'91fertile ground\u8217\'92, but in the Solitant tradition it means \u8216\'91true artist\u8217\'92.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Interesting,\u8221\'94 Mandri lied.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I\u8217\'92ll tell you what\u8217\'92s interesting,\u8221\'94 Vigram said, lifting his thick brows. \u8220\'93The old witch who gave that sermon, she said that art has nothing to do with what a person makes. Have you ever heard that? I didn\u8217\'92t understand it at first, but you know, it\u8217\'92s true. True artistry, consider this, true artistry is a state of mind. Some of the greatest artists never made anything at all.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri stuffed his mouth with a breadroll in a futile attempt to stifle himself. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I was under the impression,\u8221\'94 he said as he worked the roll into his cheek, \u8220\'93that an artist makes art.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram frowned and tutted. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Nobody makes anything,\u8221\'94 he insisted. \u8220\'93We can only ever rearrange. When you paint, you push pigment around. How is that any different than my footprints rearranging sand? No, either everyone is an artist, or, and this is my claim, an artist is a certain kind}{\f1\fs24\b0\i1  }{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 of}{\f1\fs24\b0\i1  }{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 person. A certain kind of }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 mind}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 . You know, too many big ideas, too many shallow excuses, that sort of thing. It\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s a mind marked by excess\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 not abundance\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 excess}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 .\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Don\u8217\'92t look at me,\u8221\'94 Mandri said.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram smiled. The fat swerve of his bottom lip was a baited hook. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93So exaggerated is the artist\u8217\'92s sense of self that it becomes impossible to describe them without resorting to sarcasm.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You\u8217\'92re still looking at me.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s smile swerved into a bully\u8217\'92s smirk. \u8220\'93There is no one else here.\u8221\'94 }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Two slaves stood at attention by the kitchen\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s double-swing doors. Vigram\u8217\'92s blindness to their existence was cruel, but this did not dismantle his point, as Mandri did think highly of himself.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Why shouldn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t he?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 When he was ten, he could paint a portrait in oils that rivaled the old Jeketian masters. These weren\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t polish-jobs where the paint was thin and every hair was rendered into being like he\u8217\'92d lost the plot of reality. By his hand, a single stroke of umber was enough to define the chestnut skin, the high-arched cheek, the skull underneath, the light, the shadow, even the mood of the piece. Warms and cools in one single stroke! With twenty-four, he could find a vivid likeness. There might be a few extra nibbles to make a beauty\u8217\'92s eyes glisten and her cleavage heave, but nobody counted those. Well, Vigram counted them. Lascivious men counted them. Probably, every man counted them. Some women, presumably.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 And like any prideful artist, Mandri would strut around the grand Hrkan galleries and make clear his contempt for his contemporaries. He\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d stand in front of a painting and say, \u8220\'93This piece really moves me,\u8221\'94 and he\u8217\'92d make a show of walking away. All must bear witness to his mop of black hair as it flounced itself in time with his skinny, stiff-backed step. Or he\u8217\'92d say, \u8220\'93I weep at every stroke,\u8221\'94 and he\u8217\'92d flash a knowing smile. Back in those days, he\u8217\'92d say these things directly to a fellow artist\u8217\'92s face if he could get to it. He never thought of it as bullying because artists, like himself, were safe targets for playful derision; it wasn\u8217\'92t the person he was making fun of, it was their failed attempts to express themselves, and those were }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 completely}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  different things. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d never go after the person.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 No, never.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Artists were the best people. They needed tough skin, unearned confidence, and a flair for the dramatic. Without any of that, an artist expires in obscurity. Then there is the servility. Above all, an artist must be servile to the hand that feeds. And look at that, Vigram had a lot of food at his table.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Vigram,\u8221\'94 Mandri said confidently, \u8220\'93you are an idiot.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram choked on a wad of smushed up leech. He coughed, pounded his chest, then coughed again. Once recovered, he glared at Mandri with protuberant eyes, oblivious to the brown specks of food caught in his long beard. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93In whose house do we sit?!\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Yours, of course.\u8221\'94 Mandri said as he tried to muster a disarming smile. \u8220\'93But when it comes to these things, when it comes to matters of art, I am correct. If I am not correct, then you certainly aren\u8217\'92t.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Why? Because I like some painters who aren\u8217\'92t you?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I don\u8217\'92t paint anymore, so I don\u8217\'92t care who you like.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93If you\u8217\'92re not a painter, then why do I pay you for lessons?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93These lessons are a charity.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Mandri, I }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 pay}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  you!\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Even so.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Leech juice dripped down the corner of Vigram\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s lip, accenting his frown. He wiped it away, then broke into a jolly smile. \u8220\'93Hah!\u8221\'94 he exclaimed, and he stabbed his clam shell into a bowl of fermented leech. So much lard was smashed into those leeches that the gray paste could grease a wheel. Vigram\u8217\'92s outstretched tongue batted at the stringy slop before he slumped it all down his gullet. He patted his fat lips dry with his fifth napkin, then threw that napkin onto the floor.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93We both know,\u8221\'94 he said, \u8220\'93that you only come here for the money.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Then we are both mistaken.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram rolled his eyes and set his leech lard mush aside. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Listen,\u8221\'94 he said. He put on his business face. It looked something like a spooked coconut. \u8220\'93You have a gift. I should say that I believe in your talent. You should believe me when I say that success is there for the taking. I know you don\u8217\'92t want to debase yourself by doing what it takes to succeed, but the money is easy. It is }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 sitting}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  there for the taking. People }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 want}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  to give it to you.\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94 }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri settled into his chair because he knew what was coming next: Vigram\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s life story. It always came out as a sort of instructional, as if Mandri could do it, too.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The first step in the accumulation of wealth was often left out because not everyone could easily murder their father to inherit a sizable sum. That being said, four ounces of silver would do.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Next, loan those four ounces out to a leech farm. Why a leech farm? Beyond the blunt symbolism, there\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s not much of a reason. It is important to remember that not every leech farm is the same. Look for a farm that supplies a Solitant temple with leeches for their bloodletting rituals. That\u8217\'92s a surefire bet because temples are always the last thing the mob will torch in lean times. When the lean times hit especially hard, when the only bread available is made from fern-roots, the leech farm will feel the squeeze. They\u8217\'92ll ask for leniency on the interest rate. Do not budge. Take over the farm. The leechmen might accuse you of committing usury against Solraheen Himself, because in some small way you are, but Solraheen is dead and so what does it matter? Get together with a priest and pray about it around a jug of beer, flash him a slip of silver, and the bleary shine will blind him to all the world\u8217\'92s iniquity.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 With the farm in hand, begin to lend smaller amounts over shorter terms to farmers who need emergency loans to pay their taxes after droughts or infestations. Split these debts up and sell them off in Meskalim\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s bond market. There\u8217\'92s not much money in Meskalim itself, but it has a busy port and that means access to all the important markets of the Hrkan world.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 From there, it\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s a simple matter of reducing your eyes to tiny pinpricks, ridding yourself of limbs, segmenting the body, and now that you\u8217\'92ve become a leech entirely, you may attach firmly the to the continent\u8217\'92s rump and drink your fill. Anyone who tries to shake you off before you\u8217\'92ve finished becomes your slave. You pick out the salvageable fools for your own use, and now you\u8217\'92ve got a brood of leeches pouring over lists of sums credited and debts owed. So long as you maintain a layer of abstraction between these creatures and your henchmen, it\u8217\'92s all self-sustaining.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 And that\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s it.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Now you have a comfortable life.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Idle hours.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93No debasement necessary,\u8221\'94 Vigram added before licking his}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1  plump fingers clean of sun-dried leech oil. This oil was a true delicacy, squeezed as it was from the salivary glands of leeches aged twenty years or more. Leeches that big are more devourers than bloodsuckers, so they\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92re much more expensive to feed.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Now,\u8221\'94 Vigram said. \u8220\'93}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0 You know all about the making of a painting. That\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s why I respect you. I know all about the making of money. At a certain point you have to join them up, because the world doesn\u8217\'92t work how it should. The world works how it works. You }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 have}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  to make a living. Success, Mandri, is a very sweet thing.\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He clapped his meaty hands, prompting one of the nameless slaves to come and take his plates. Once done, he stood up. His chair protested the sudden shift of his massive weight with a terrible squeak.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93We should begin,\u8221\'94 he said.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri nodded, then wiped his own mouth and excused himself from the table. He took his time, because he wasn\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92t looking forward to beginning anything. But, he had to. He needed the money.  He followed Vigram out to the vine-trellised, brown-brick patio which offered a grand view of Vigram\u8217\'92s leech lakes.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Twelve massive man-made leech lakes tiled Vigram\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s large estate, each a perfect square. The rim of the nearest squares were lined with roofless stalls, with each stall penning a live animal into the shallows. Goats, horses, cows; any animal larger than a large dog would do. They were each of them slumped onto support beams. Their snouts were tied into bags of intoxicating freeweed smoke. This was not an act of beneficence to inure them to the pain, it was Vigram\u8217\'92s way of keeping his peaceful country air free from their wild moaning.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The rest of the estate was mostly flies and marshgrass. The sky was cloudy with the kind of tall, mountainous clouds that swallowed landscape paintings whole, the kind that cast broad shadows down upon the world to pull the viewer through the distance, as they pulled Mandri now, all the way out to the sparkling blue line on the horizon which was the sea.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram set himself to work in setting up an easel which he claimed to have built himself. Slaves cut and planed the lumber, brewed the glue, hammered the metal fittings, but Vigram\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s mighty mind designed it.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Are you sure you want to do this here?\u8221\'94 Mandri asked.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Why not here?\u8221\'94 said Vigram.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri eyed the distressed animals in the lakes below. Shaved down to the pink skin like criminals, they\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d been reduced to bags of blood. Leechmen waded in the waters between the stalls so that they could trowel off the largest leeches and toss them into grass baskets. Those leeches which weren\u8217\'92t engaged in the constant struggle for the cleanest spot of flesh were busy rippling the water\u8217\'92s surface with their mating. It was a bit of an orgy down there. If only those foul creatures knew that their bliss would end with their bellies being split-open, fried, and served on a plate.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri felt his stomach turn. On came a pang of guilt, because all those goats, horses, and cows were suffering in leech paradise on account of him. He\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92d eaten more than his fill of Meskalimi leech. Who was he to judge Vigram? What right did he have to feel compassion for those animals when his own stomach was full of leech?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Well,\u8221\'94 Mandri said as he eyed the patio\u8217\'92s trellis. \u8220\'93These vines aren\u8217\'92t yet thick enough for a solid shadow. A dappled light will fall upon the sitter. It\u8217\'92s going to make things difficult.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He moved into the dappled shade to demonstrate the effect on his own skin.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93My dear Mandriiii,\u8221\'94 Vigram said, his pained drawl rolling heavily. \u8220\'93The patterns of light and shadow are a magical vesture.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Well,\u8221\'94 Mandri pondered, \u8220\'93I might suggest that you worry about \u8216\'91magical vestures\u8217\'92 when you\u8217\'92re skilled enough to make a face look like a face.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93No,\u8221\'94 Vigram said firmly, long beard wiggling. \u8220\'93I won\u8217\'92t have it any other way. A magical vesture for a magical subject.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 As if bidden from the heavens by Vigram\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s words, Vigram\u8217\'92s slave, Pel, joined them on the patio. She was Vigram\u8217\'92s favorite subject. If it weren\u8217\'92t for her, every painting would have been a self-portrait. Younger than Vigram, older than Mandri, she was thin, severe, and beautiful, one of those women who seem impossible to touch. Hardly anyone knew of her existence because Vigram kept her squirreled away. A prize. The man stared at her like he was about stamp a return on interest.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Isn\u8217\'92t she a subject fit for holy oils?\u8221\'94 Vigram asked, squeezing out a suggestive smile.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Undoubtedly,\u8221\'94 Mandri said, and rare for him, he meant it. Pel was cold but comely, with clear skin, sun-kissed cheeks, and long brown hair falling over her shoulder. Her plain robe was spun from a host of earthy threads, browns and reds, waste-material, together affecting a dark russet. A very broad neckline. And she was mute, or at least, Mandri assumed she was, because he\u8217\'92d never heard her speak a word. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Her wide, bright eyes caught him staring.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri smiled through a mask of amicability. His instinct was to flee out into the leech lakes, but all he could do was stand there while his legs began to sweat.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93White light!\u8221\'94 Vigram roared as he rushed to the edge of the patio. \u8220\'93Quiet that animal!\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The target of his ire was an old, yellow headed ox. It was mooing. Angry, stuck inside its stall in the nearest leech lake, it mooed again. It bucked against its restraints, then mooed with a desperate, plaintive lilt.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Silence that beast!\u8221\'94 Vigram roared. His leechmen scurried into action. \u8220\'93And you,\u8221\'94 Vigram said to Mandri, \u8220\'93help her onto her stool. I\u8217\'92ll ready the canvas and paint.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri sighed a sigh for the old ox. He privately resolved to never eat a Meskalimi leech again. Then he did what he was told and helped Pel up onto a high-legged stool. Then he waited for Vigram to settle down, which he did not do until the ox\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s moos quieted down. The lesson began when Vigram picked up stick of soft charcoal and confronted the empty canvas.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The first exploratory marks were always difficult. Every work of art must be built on a firm foundation; get it wrong and everything that comes after will be wrong as well. Unfortunately for Vigram, he had that all too common beginner\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s habit of refusing to use his god damned brain. He started drawing wherever his hand happened to land, and whatever happened next was up to Solraheen. The end result was that Pel\u8217\'92s head was half the size it needed to be. It was also too left-of-center. There\u8217\'92d be an awkward expanse behind her flowing hair.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Hold on,\u8221\'94 said Mandri. \u8220\'93You\u8217\'92re moving too fast. Don\u8217\'92t think of this as a portrait\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 not yet. All we have right now is an oval, a rectangular canvas, and where that oval sits on that canvas. All you\u8217\'92re doing is putting an oval on a rectangle.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I know.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Then do it. Think about, where\u8217\'92s the best place to put that oval? How big should it be? Remember, you have the whole canvas there.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You\u8217\'92ve told me all this before.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram rubbed the marks away with his palm.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He stepped back.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He studied Pel.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He stepped again to the canvas, then scratched another black oval exactly where the first had been.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Is that better?\u8221\'94 he asked with all the awareness of a potted plant. \u8220\'93You think it needs to be bigger?\u8221\'94 He drew the oval again, but only slightly larger. From then on, every other mark he made was accompanied by a look back to Mandri for reassurance. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Don\u8217\'92t look at me,\u8221\'94 said Mandri, frustrated. \u8220\'93Look at her.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93But I need to know if the marks I make are correct.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93They\u8217\'92re never correct.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 There was a glimmer of understanding in Vigram\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s eye, but just as soon as it came it faded. \u8220\'93If the mark is always wrong, then how could I ever move past it? I\u8217\'92ll be redrawing the same line over and over again.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You move on when you can\u8217\'92t stand to try anymore.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93That sounds miserable.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93It is,\u8221\'94 Mandri said, feeling understood. \u8220\'93You should be defeated by the time you\u8217\'92ve finished. I\u8217\'92ve always thought that you should never feel good about what you\u8217\'92re doing, because that means you\u8217\'92re not trying hard enough. Now, finish\u8230\'85finish the basic shapes, at least.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Alright.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91finished\u8217\'92 the \u8216\'91basic shapes\u8217\'92. He looked to Mandri for final approval.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93It\u8217\'92s your paint,\u8221\'94 Mandri said with a shrug. \u8220\'93But you need to understand the importance of the drawing. You\u8217\'92re always drawing. Even when painting, you\u8217\'92re drawing.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I know,\u8221\'94 Vigram whined. \u8220\'93You talk about it all the time.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram made to redraw the too-large chin, but was interrupted by a loud, mournful ox bellow.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Damn it!\u8221\'94 Vigram cried, shaking his fist.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Please,\u8221\'94 Mandri sighed. \u8220\'93Focus. Don\u8217\'92t get distracted. Let your men do their job.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93If they were doing their job, I wouldn\u8217\'92t have heard that noise!\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Please,\u8221\'94 Mandri repeated. \u8220\'93Focus.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram closed his eyes and took a deep breath. When he next looked upon his canvas, he was resolved: \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I\u8217\'92m done drawing! I\u8217\'92m here to paint!\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He tossed his charcoal over his shoulder and picked up his favorite brush. He dipped it into a bluish shade of bronze, then threw down the first splotches of color in a flurry. It was an affront to the senses. Like a notary to an execution, Mandri was getting paid to witness it. And Sol\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s light, that bentberry fiber canvas was innocent. Expensive, too, a weave coveted for its depth of feeling, for how it made the undertones blush. Assaulted by Vigram\u8217\'92s hand, those tones were bruised and near to bleeding. It was a very confused slapping around.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri had to look away.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He glanced in Pel\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s direction.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The moment his eyes wandered over her exposed shoulder, the corner of her lip perked up. Feeling accused of something, Mandri grunted. But then, why should he feel accused? Why shouldn't he look? Why else would she wear that lilac scent? Why bare her neck? Why bite that honeyed, aching lip? Why, if Solraheen Himself was not inciting him to stare, why did the soft shadows of the vines overhead frame her hazel eyes with the most magnificent golden light? A magical vesture indeed. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Mandri, stop flirting with my muse.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I am not flirting,\u8221\'94 Mandri claimed. \u8220\'93I\u8217\'92m checking proportions.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Yes, and how }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 are}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  her proportions?\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The old goat\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s eyebrows jumped suggestively. Pel sighed in annoyance. The yellow headed ox weighed in with an oxen roar.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93MRRRRRUUUUUUUUUUUUU!\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93That\u8217\'92s it!\u8221\'94 Vigram shouted. Below, in the leech lake, two leechmen tried to force the yellow-headed ox\u8217\'92s snout into a bag of freeweed smoke. \u8220\'93That\u8217\'92s IT!\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram rushed to the edge of the patio. He was just in time to see the ox buck both leechmen off, break its constraints, then jump its rail. The beast tore through the leech lake, kicking up waves of confused, copulating leeches.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Stop that ox!\u8221\'94 Vigram shouted as he pointed with his loaded brush. \u8220\'93There\u8217\'92s a five silver-strings of leeches attached to that beast! If you don\u8217\'92t\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 \u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Vigram,\u8221\'94 Mandri said firmly. \u8220\'93You\u8217\'92ll make a bad habit if you stop in the middle of the work.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Don\u8217\'92t tell me what to do, boy! On }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 my}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  farm!?\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You\u8217\'92re rich enough already. Let it go.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram drew up in rage. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Don\u8217\'92t ask me to let go of my principles. That I will not do. That animal }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 defies}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  me!\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram stomped back towards the canvas and set his brush down on the palette, not caring that it rolled into a swatch of blue. Ever the man of action, he marched down to the leech lakes to order other men into action. He screamed at them until his bald head rumpled and turned red. But his screaming worked against him, for it stirred a few leeched cows and goats out of their drug induced reverie. Their horrid shrieks woke up others, who shrieked in turn, and now a cascade of doleful whinnies, bleats, snorts, and muleish hee-haws pricked the ear. Elevated into a higher rage which rivaled thunder, Vigram yelled right back at them.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri picked up Vigram\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s dirty brush. He twirled its horse-hair head through the muddy colors of the palette. There was enough untouched pigment for at least four more of these awkward lessons. Very expensive. It almost felt like he was committing a crime by running the brush through an untouched slug of yellow. He tskd his tongue, picked up a wooden shim, then moved to scrape that yellow back into its little clay jar.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Uncomfortable?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 It took Mandri a moment to pick the source of the question from out of the cacophony of animal screams. It was Pel. Stern and measured, her voice carried none of the motherly lilt heard in his dreams.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Uhhh,\u8221\'94 he muttered as he matched her gaze. \u8220\'93This isn\u8217\'92t exactly paradise.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93No,\u8221\'94 she said simply. \u8220\'93It is not.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93You know, I didn\u8217\'92t know you could speak.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Pel crossed her arms and simply stared at him, unblinking. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Right,\u8221\'94 he said. His legs were sweating fiercely now. He looked around for Vigram\u8217\'92s coinpurse. Vigram never bothered hiding it; the man wanted people to admire its heft. And there it was on a window sill, sitting like leech too fat to crawl.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He picked it up, loosed the drawstring, and pulled out one full silver string of coin. He tumbled it around in his cupped hand. Pel watched him intently.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I\u8217\'92m only taking what I\u8217\'92m owed,\u8221\'94 he said to her, and then he took a few coins more than what he was owed.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93What, }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 precisely}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  are you owed?\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8221\'94 she asked.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri held up the short stack of coin in his hand. \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93A quarter silver string.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Pel\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s smirk reappeared. \u8220\'93My dear,\u8221\'94 she said as his spine shivered, \u8220\'93you are owed quite a bit more than that.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I don\u8217\'92t want Vigram to be any more upset than he already is,\u8221\'94 Mandri said. \u8220\'93I don\u8217\'92t want to end up tied down in one of his leech lakes. He\u8217\'92s got a nasty side to him.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Vigram only has the one side.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93He doesn\u8217\'92t abuse you or anything, does he?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 The question was stupid because Vigram, at present, was chasing a leechman with a grass whip.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Do you care?\u8221\'94 Pel asked with a puckered brow. \u8220\'93What would you do if I said that he does?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93I don\u8217\'92t know,\u8221\'94 Mandri said. \u8220\'93I guess you\u8217\'92re right.\u8221\'94 He put Vigram\u8217\'92s coinpurse back where he\u8217\'92d found it. He wasn\u8217\'92t interested in lingering any longer than he needed to. He had plans.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Vigram was right about you.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93What\u8217\'92s that?\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93 \u8216\'91A thin veneer lidding a black pit of shame.\u8217\'92 That was how he described you.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Yeah, well\u8230\'85same to you.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri hurried down the patio\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s steps, refusing to acknowledge Pel\u8217\'92s amusement. Then he thought better of it and gave a polite smile over his shoulder. Then he hurried down Vigram\u8217\'92s lane, which was the most terrifying lane because it was sandwiched between two leech lakes.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri kept to the right. The lake on the left held leeches bigger than his arm. Instead of stalls penning in live animals, its banks were lined with bones. The lake\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s center was a constant swirl as hundreds of leeches swarmed just under the water\u8217\'92s surface, bunch-crawling like caterpillars, slithering like eels, desperate to get in a taste of whatever corpse had bothered to drop in. And the moment Mandri grimaced, a round shape popped out of that writhing mass like a crab-trap buoy. It rotated around, revealing itself to be a severed head, somewhat fresh, a human head. Close cropped hair. An upturned nose. A mouth open in a silent scream. A leech latched onto its cheek, and as soon as that had come, it sank.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Nope!\u8221\'94 Mandri said to himself. \u8220\'93Nope, nope, nope,\u8221\'94 he repeated as he darted towards Vigram\u8217\'92s gate. Once onto the road, he quickened his pace. He didn\u8217\'92t feel out of reach until he crested the big hill that overlooked Vigram\u8217\'92s estate. Only then did he risk a look back.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Vigram and his squad of leechmen were chasing the ox\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s pink rump as it boucned through the marshgrass. It seemed lose them when it dove into hedge. Meanwhile, Pel stood on the patio. She stared straight through the distance, right at Mandri, like a ghost.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He pretended not to see her.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He walked all the way to Geem\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s and used his pay to buy half a bushel of burgeons. They were down-valley burgeons, decent stuff, but the waxy capsule leaves were liable to split. The insides would dry out before he could get to them in the ways that he wanted to. But, that was all Geem had, so he bought them.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He continued along the road, stomping his way through the muddy wheel-ruts, slapping away the flies, humming nursery rhymes, trying anything to distract himself from overthinking the sight of that severed head. Because it was only a severed head. That\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s all it was! It might have never belonged to a body, or been a person. There\u8217\'92s no way of knowing, really. Anything was possible. Besides, a rich man like Vigram was sure to accumulate enemies alongside the rest of his possessions. What was one little head? People were dying all the time.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 Mandri thought about his barren farm.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He thought about relaxing under his tree.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He thought about sipping a milky hooch spiked through with burgeon juice.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 He would like to escape into that hooch. It would be bliss.}}